{
  "title": "tolist() does not graph when capture_scalar_outputs = False",
  "issue_id": "163798",
  "bug_description": "repo:\n```\n@torch.compile(fullgraph=False, backend=\"eager\")\ndef func(a):\n    u0, u1 = a.tolist()\n    return a*u0*u1\nfunc(torch.tensor([1,2]))\n```\n\n\noutput graph:\n```\nclass GraphModule(torch.nn.Module):\n    def forward(self, L_a_: \"i64[2][1]cpu\"):\n        l_a_ = L_a_\n        \n         # File: /home/lsakka/pytorch/pytorch/example.py:7 in func, code: u0, u1 = a.tolist()\n        getitem = l_a_[0]\n        item: \"Sym(u0)\" = getitem.item();  getitem = None\n        getitem_1 = l_a_[1]\n        item_1: \"Sym(u1)\" = getitem_1.item();  getitem_1 = None\n        \n         # File: /home/lsakka/pytorch/pytorch/example.py:8 in func, code: return a*u0*u1\n        mul: \"i64[2][1]cpu\" = l_a_ * item;  l_a_ = item = None\n        mul_1: \"i64[2][1]cpu\" = mul * item_1;  mul = item_1 = None\n        return (mul_1,)\n        \n```\nin contrast, the following program does graph break.\n```\n@torch.compile(fullgraph=False, backend=\"eager\")\ndef func2(a):\n    u0 = a.item()\n    return a*u0\n```\nfunc2(torch.tensor([1]))\n\n\n\nThis is problematic because now we have item() in the graphs then we might re-trace \nin autograd and fail because capture_scalar_outputs=False and its too late to know where to graph break!\nseen this recently with Ads cc @chauhang @penguinwu @ezyang @bobrenjc93 @pianpwk \n\nNote that with inductor backend fails to compile !\n```\n@torch.compile(fullgraph=False, backend=\"inductor\")\ndef func(a):\n    u0, u1 = a.tolist()\n    return a*u0*u1\nfunc(torch.tensor([1,2]))\n```\na proposed [fix](https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/pull/163807) but need to wait for CI and add unit test. \ncc @chauhang @penguinwu @ezyang @bobrenjc93",
